1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,060
 Picture the usual map of prehistory, people leave Africa, spread through Asia, and cross

2
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:06,500
 into the Americas.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:11,360
 It is a useful sketch, but sketches can hide whole populations between their arrows.

4
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:16,160
 In Siberia, the grave of one small child exposed one of those missing stories.

5
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:20,920
 His DNA connected places we have been taught to imagine separately, the place is Malta,

6
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:26,200
 near Lake Baikol, where archaeologists found an ice age settlement and a child's burial.

7
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:31,160
 He lived about 24,000 years ago, long before farming cities or written borders.

8
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:36,540
 The site held carved ivory objects and ornaments, traces of skill and social life.

9
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:39,080
 For decades the burial was remarkable.

10
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:42,840
 Then scientists learned to read the DNA still held in ancient bone.

11
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:47,000
 Ancient DNA does not tell us the child's name, language or favourite story.

12
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,760
 It lets researchers compare inherited patterns with those in other ancient and living people.

13
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:55,680
 Think of it as finding shared spelling mistakes in copies of a very old book.

14
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:59,400
 The more patterns two groups share, the more their histories may overlap.

15
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,420
 Though the details still need testing.

16
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:03,680
 The Malta genome produced a surprise.

17
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:06,800
 It shared ancestry with indigenous peoples of the Americas.

18
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:11,240
 It also showed deep genetic links to ancient populations farther west in Eurasia.

19
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:15,440
 A Siberian child suddenly sat near the centre of a much larger family tree.

20
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:17,720
 The old map needed more than a longer arrow.

21
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:19,760
 It needed a new branch.

22
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:23,720
 Researchers call this broad ancestral lineage, ancient north Eurasian.

23
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:26,240
 That is a genetic label, not a nation.

24
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:31,120
 The child was one member of a population, not the ancestor of everyone who came later.

25
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:35,600
 Nor does a shared genetic signal mean people cross directly from Europe to America.

26
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:40,880
 It means ancestors related to his population once lived across a wider northern world.

27
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:44,360
 Siberia in the Ice Age was not an empty white margin on the map.

28
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:49,440
 People hunted, made tools, cared for children and adapted to severe cold.

29
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:54,720
 Other ancient genomes from northeastern Siberia reveal related, but distinct, populations

30
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:56,480
 living there even earlier.

31
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:01,200
 The region was a long inhabited crossroads, not just a waiting room before the Americas.

32
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:04,800
 Now imagine generations moving across that immense landscape.

33
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:09,880
 Some families stay, others follow animals, rivers, seasons, or opportunities we can no

34
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:11,280
 longer see.

35
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:13,000
 Groups meet and have children.

36
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,640
 Their descendants carry pieces of both histories.

37
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:20,840
 Over thousands of years, a population can leave traces far from any grave we have found.

38
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:25,520
 To the east, descendants of this northern ancestry, mixed with people carrying East Asian

39
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:30,240
 related ancestry, that mixture was part of the deep history of the founding populations

40
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:31,600
 of the Americas.

41
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:36,080
 It happened in Asia, long before they were borders named Russia, Canada, or the United

42
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:37,080
 States.

43
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:41,240
 So the first Americans did not arrive as a simple, unmixed branch from a single modern

44
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:42,840
 population.

45
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:47,840
 During the last Ice Age, lower seas exposed land between Northeast Asia and Alaska.

46
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:50,240
 We call that broad region Beringia.

47
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:53,480
 It was habitat, not a narrow bridge with a queue.

48
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:57,080
 People could live, move, and change there over many generations.

49
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:01,400
 How and when the founding populations traveled onward remains an active question.

50
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:04,680
 The genetic signal is visible far beyond the crossing itself.

51
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:10,400
 A child buried in what is now Montana, about 12,600 years ago, carried Malta related

52
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:11,720
 ancestry.

53
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:15,600
 His genome is closely related to indigenous peoples of the Americas.

54
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,760
 By his lifetime, that northern mixture was already part of an American story.

55
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:21,480
 There is a trap here.

56
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:24,600
 Genetic kinship is not the same thing as cultural identity.

57
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:29,280
 The Malta child was not a European, and indigenous Americans did not become European through

58
00:03:29,280 --> 00:03:31,120
 ancient ancestry.

59
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:33,360
 Modern labels belong to much later histories.

60
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,800
 They cannot be pasted onto Ice Age people.

61
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:41,320
 Their descendants formed distinct communities, languages, and ways of life across the Americas.

62
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:42,800
 Now turn the map around.

63
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:46,400
 The same northern ancestry also left a mark to the west.

64
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:51,960
 Ancient DNA from Europe shows that its population history was never a single unbroken line.

65
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:56,360
 Early farmers, western hunter-gatherers, and people with northern Eurasian-related ancestry

66
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:58,080
 all contributed.

67
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:01,320
 Europe's later populations were made through meetings between groups with different

68
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:02,480
 pasts.

69
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:06,880
 One route ran through Eastern European hunter-gatherers, who carried some ancestry related

70
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:07,880
 to Malta.

71
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:11,560
 They were separated from him by enormous distance and thousands of years.

72
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:13,440
 They were not copies of his people.

73
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:16,160
 Each generation had its own mixture in history.

74
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:20,220
 Yet that faint inherited connection helped explain a missing piece in Europe's genetic

75
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:21,220
 puzzle.

76
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:25,360
 Much later, communities on the step north of the black and caspian seas formed through

77
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:27,120
 further mixing.

78
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:31,520
 Pastoralist groups associated with the Amnaya spread across parts of Europe around 5,000

79
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:32,520
 years ago.

80
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:36,600
 Their ancestry included a contribution from Eastern hunter-gatherers, which carried that

81
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:38,320
 older northern thread.

82
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:42,600
 The signal traveled west again, now inside a very different population.

83
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:47,760
 Ancient genomes show that this step movement changed ancestry in many European regions.

84
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:50,600
 It did not erase everyone who was already there.

85
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:53,600
 People met, mixed, and lived on.

86
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:58,160
 Archaeologists also track changing burial customs, tools, and settlement patterns.

87
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:04,000
 DNA reveals movement of people, while objects and landscapes help us ask how they lived.

88
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:09,080
 Added step ancestry later moved eastward to reaching parts of Central and South Asia.

89
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:13,400
 But South Asian history is much older and more layered than that one movement.

90
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:17,240
 Ancient populations there had already formed through other deep encounters.

91
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:22,520
 A small northern genetic thread can be real without becoming the whole story of a continent.

92
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:27,800
 This is why the headline version, one lost tribe conquering continents, would be misleading.

93
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:32,080
 The evidence points to related ancestry's moving and mixing through multiple populations

94
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:34,040
 over immense time.

95
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:38,600
 Some links are direct enough to model, others are inferred from incomplete samples.

96
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:44,400
 The exciting discovery is a network of relationships where an old map showed isolated boxes.

97
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:48,680
 Ancient DNA works best when we can compare people from different places and times.

98
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:50,440
 One genome gives a point.

99
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:55,240
 Many genomes begin to show a moving pattern, but the ground has preserved only a tiny, uneven

100
00:05:55,240 --> 00:05:57,200
 sample of everyone who lived.

101
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:01,600
 A new burial can shift the picture, especially in places where almost no ancient DNA

102
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:03,160
 survives.

103
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:06,240
 Even the word ancestor can make the story sound too tidy.

104
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:08,880
 The multer child is a useful genetic reference.

105
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:11,920
 A window into people related to later ancestors.

106
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:14,760
 He may never have travelled far from the river where he lived.

107
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:20,000
 The connections we see were carried by countless others, most of whom left no sampled bones.

108
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,560
 Nor can genes tell us which stories these people told around the fire.

109
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:27,400
 They cannot recover the names they used for themselves, or what their children feared and

110
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:28,400
 loved.

111
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:33,040
 A carved object can tell us about skill and imagination, but its meaning is not automatically

112
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:34,040
 clear.

113
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:39,200
 Prehistory becomes richer when DNA, archaeology, and living communities are heard together.

114
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:41,720
 There is another reason to handle this carefully.

115
00:06:41,720 --> 00:06:43,760
 Ancient human remains are not just data.

116
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:48,440
 They belong to people, and discoveries can matter deeply to descendant communities.

117
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:52,680
 Research is strongest when it treats those communities as partners with knowledge and rights.

118
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:57,800
 A striking genetic result should deepen respect, not turn anyone's ancestry into a spectacle.

119
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:01,160
 So what exactly did the Siberian burial change?

120
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:05,760
 It showed that an ancient population in Northern Eurasia was connected to ancestors of indigenous

121
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:06,760
 Americans.

122
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:11,480
 It helped reveal a related ancestral layer in Europe, later carried and reshaped by

123
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:17,000
 more migrations, and it made Siberia visible as a centre of human history, not a blank space

124
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,240
 between destinations.

125
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:22,200
 We still cannot draw every path these families took.

126
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:27,240
 The climate changed, coastlines moved, and populations formed and disappeared.

127
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:31,840
 Some routes may wait under water, or in soil no one has sampled, but the broad lesson

128
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:33,440
 is already clear.

129
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:36,640
 Human history is full of meetings we once could not see.

130
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:40,280
 Imagine standing at Malta before anyone knew what a genome was.

131
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:44,640
 You would see a child buried beside an ice age settlement, and a world that seemed impossibly

132
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:45,640
 remote.

133
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:50,520
 Now that same life opens questions, stretching from Siberia to the Americas and across

134
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:52,000
 Western Eurasia.

135
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:53,720
 The distance did not vanish.

136
00:07:53,720 --> 00:07:56,640
 We finally learnt to see the connections across it.

137
00:07:56,640 --> 00:08:00,040
 The lost migration was never one march by one named people.

138
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:04,680
 It was a long sequence of movement, mixing, and survival across Northern Asia.

139
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:09,640
 Its descendants carried inherited traces east into the Americas, and west into later Eurasian

140
00:08:09,640 --> 00:08:10,960
 populations.

141
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:14,920
 What was lost was our view of the connections, until an ancient child helped bring them

142
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:15,920
 back.

143
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:18,480
 That is the real rewrite.

144
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:21,640
 Contonents were never sealed chapters of separate human stories.

145
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:25,400
 People moved through landscapes that looked different from ours, carrying relationships

146
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:27,040
 we could not yet trace.

147
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:31,440
 Each new ancient genome adds a few lines to that unfinished map, and somewhere beneath

148
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:34,920
 the next patch of ground, another quiet life may change it again.

